1. Registers.java
Stores the CPU registers and their values. It helps the CPU quickly access and update important data.

2. Memory.java
Represents the memory used by the PIC16F72 simulator. 
It stores program instructions and data values.

3. Instruction.java
Represents one instruction, such as MOVLW, ADDWF, or BCF. It stores the instruction name, operands, and original instruction text.

4. ExecutionResult.java
Stores the result after an instruction is executed. It can contain information such as the result value, status changes, or execution details.

5. InstructionSet.java
Contains the instructions supported by the simulator.
It helps the CPU identify and execute the correct operation.

6. CPU.java
Acts as the main processor of the simulator. It performs Fetch → Decode → Execute and updates registers, memory, and flags.

7. SimulatorUI.java
Provides the graphical interface for interacting with the simulator. It contains buttons like Load, Reset, Step, and Run.

8. Main.java
This is the starting point of the Java program. 
It launches the simulator and starts the user interface or CLI.

9. Compile
All Java files are compiled to check for errors and convert the code into executable bytecode. 
If compilation succeeds, we can run the simulator.

10. Test CLI/Core
First, we test the CPU and simulator logic through the command line.
This makes sure the core functionality works before using the GUI.

11. Open GUI
After the core is working correctly, we open the graphical simulator interface. The GUI allows us to control and observe the CPU easily.

12. LOAD
The LOAD button loads the program instructions into the simulator's memory. The program is then ready to be executed.

13. RESET
RESET brings the CPU back to its initial state. Registers, program counter, and other required values are reset.

14. STEP
STEP executes only one instruction at a time.
This helps us observe exactly what happens during each CPU cycle.

15. STEP
Pressing STEP again executes the next instruction. 
We can check how the registers, memory, PC, and flags change.

16. RUN
RUN continuously executes the loaded instructions. The simulator continues until the program finishes or reaches a stopping condition.

17. SLEEP TERMINATED
This indicates that the CPU's sleep state has ended or execution has been stopped. 
The simulator can then continue or display the final CPU state.

Therefore, this architecture shows how all the components work together to simulate the PIC16F72 CPU,
from loading and executing instructions to updating registers, memory, and status flags.
